course: everyday
lesson: 249
title: Cricket, rugby, basketball, hockey and swimming
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A cricket ball is very hard, almost like a stone covered in leather, so batters wear a helmet, pads and gloves, and fielders close to the bat wear a helmet or stand back.
explain: Each sport has rules that also keep people safe: in rugby you pass only backwards or sideways and tackle below the shoulders; in ice hockey the stick stays below the shoulders.
explain: In basketball you land from a jump on both feet with bent knees, and you never hang on the rim, because the hoop or you can fall.
explain: Rings, earrings and necklaces come off before sport, because they can catch and tear skin; in the pool, goggles stop the chlorine stinging your eyes.
explain: A girl in Christchurch learns rugby: her coach shows her how to tackle low around the waist and legs, and she always wears her mouthguard.
fact: A cricket ball is very hard, so batters wear a helmet, pads and gloves.
fact: Fielders close to the batter wear a helmet or stand further back, because hard shots hit them.
fact: In cricket the bowler keeps the arm straight; bending and straightening it to throw is against the rules, and the umpire (the cricket referee) will not allow it.
fact: In rugby you learn safe tackling from a coach, wear a mouthguard and tackle below the shoulders.
fact: High tackles cause head and neck injuries.
fact: In rugby the ball may be passed only backwards or sideways; a forward pass is against the rules.
fact: When you jump for a rebound (to catch the ball as it bounces off the hoop or board), you land on both feet with your knees bent to protect your ankles and knees.
fact: You do not hang on the basketball rim, because the hoop or you can fall.
fact: Rings, earrings and necklaces come off before sport, because they catch and tear skin.
fact: In ice hockey, where players chase a puck (a flat rubber disc), the stick is kept below the shoulders, because a high stick can injure faces.
fact: A mouthguard protects the teeth and mouth from knocks.
fact: Chlorine is put in pool water to kill germs.
fact: Swimming goggles stop the chlorine in pool water from stinging your eyes.
exercise: What does a cricket batter wear against a hard ball? | A helmet, pads and gloves | Only a sun hat to keep the sun out of his eyes | Only a pair of gloves | Nothing special, just his whites
exercise: Why does a cricket batter need protection? | A cricket ball is very hard | The ball is soft | The bat is very heavy | The pitch is slippery
exercise: You field very close to the batter in cricket. What should you do? | Wear a helmet or stand further back | Stand even closer so you can see the ball better | Turn your back to the batter | Field without looking at the bat, so you are not scared
exercise: How must a cricket bowler's arm move? | Straight, without throwing | Bent, like a throw | Underarm only | Any way the bowler likes
exercise: Where do you tackle in rugby? | Below the shoulders | Around the neck, to bring them down fast | At the head | By the hair or the collar of the shirt
exercise: What do you wear in your mouth for rugby? | A mouthguard | Chewing gum | A sweet | Nothing
exercise: Which way can you pass the ball in rugby? | Backwards or sideways | Only forwards, towards the try line | Any direction you like | Only straight up into the air
exercise: You jump for a rebound in basketball. How do you land? | On both feet with knees bent | On one foot with a straight leg | On your heels with locked knees | On your toes with stiff legs
exercise: After a slam dunk, a boy wants to hang on the rim. Why should he not? | The hoop could fall or he could fall | It makes the ball bounce the wrong way | The net will stretch out of shape for good | It does not count as a point unless he lets go at once
exercise: Before a netball match you are wearing earrings. What do you do? | Take them off | Tape them over loosely | Leave them in | Wear bigger ones
exercise: Why do rings come off before sport? | They catch and tear skin | They make you slower | They might change colour | Referees like plain hands
exercise: In ice hockey, where do you keep your stick? | Below your shoulders | High above your head | Pointed at the other players' faces | Behind your neck, across your shoulders
exercise: Why do many swimmers wear goggles in the pool? | So the chlorine does not sting their eyes | So that they can breathe underwater | So they float better | So the water around their face stays warm
exercise: A cricket fielder stands two metres from the bat with no helmet. What is the risk? | A hard shot could hit his head | He will miss the start of the tea break | The batter will be given out straight away | He cannot hear what the umpire says
test: In a rugby match, a player passes the ball forward to a teammate. What happens? | It is against the rules and play stops | It is allowed, but only in the second half | The team gets extra points for a clever pass | The teammate must kick it straight away
test: A boy in Brisbane tackles another player around the neck. Why is this dangerous? | High tackles cause head and neck injuries | It is far too slow to stop anyone | It makes the ball fly away | It is fine as long as the referee is not looking
test: A girl lands from a rebound on one stiff leg and hurts her knee. What should she do next time? | Land on both feet with knees bent | Jump even higher so she has more time to land | Land on her heels with straight legs | Not jump at all, so that she never lands
test: Why might a referee ask a player to remove a necklace? | It can catch and hurt the player or others | Necklaces are too shiny and dazzle the referee | It is a rule about colours | Necklaces slow the game down
test: A boy in Minnesota raises his hockey stick to his head while chasing the puck. Why is this a foul? | A high stick can injure faces | It lets him hit the puck harder | It breaks the stick | It scares the goalie
test: A bowler in a school cricket match bends his elbow and flicks the ball like a throw. What does the umpire say? | It is not allowed | It is a perfect delivery | It is worth six runs | It is out for the batter
test: Why are rugby mouthguards worn even by young players? | They protect the teeth and mouth | They help the players breathe more deeply while running | They taste nice | They keep the mouth warm on cold winter mornings
test: Why is chlorine put in pool water, even though it can sting eyes? | To kill germs, and goggles stop the stinging | To make the water a nice bright blue | To warm the water | To make swimmers float higher in the water
test: playing rugby, can i pass it forward to my mate? | No, only backwards or sideways | Yes, forward is the fastest way to score | Yes, if he's far ahead of everyone | Only in the last minute of the game
test: got my earrings in, footy starts now | Take them off first, they catch and tear skin | Leave them, they're tiny | Tape them over, that's always enough | Wear them only in the second half
test: shot the hoop n wanna hang on the rim like the pros | No, the hoop or you can fall | Yes, rims are built for hanging | Yes, for a few seconds | Only if it's a small hoop
test: my mate says chlorine in the pool is to make it blue. right? | No, chlorine kills germs in the water | Yes, it's just colouring | Yes, and it keeps the water warm too | It's there to make the water smell nice n fresh
explain: A boy in Yorkshire fielded close to the batter without a helmet; the ball hit his head and he had to go to hospital.
explain: A girl in Sydney dived into the shallow end at swimming club; she hit the bottom and hurt her neck badly.
fact: A cricket or hockey ball is hard enough to break bones, so close fielders and goalies wear helmets and pads.
fact: Most diving injuries happen in water less than about 1.5 metres (5 feet) deep, so dive only in deep water where diving is allowed.
test: wanna field right by the batter, no helmet's fine | No, wear a helmet that close | Fine if you watch the ball closely | Fine for one short over | Only if the bowler is slow n the batter is small
test: gonna dive in the shallow end, it's quicker | No, dive only in deep water where it's allowed | Yes, if you keep your hands out in front | Yes, if you jump far | Only off the side
test: Why do rugby players learn to tackle the right way? | Bad tackles can hurt necks and heads | It looks nicer for the crowd | It makes the game slower | The ball is safer when it is tackled properly
test: did u hear a kid was fielding right next to the batter with no helmet and the ball hit him in the head? real lesson? | Close fielders wear a helmet or stand back | Stand closer so the ball can't build up speed | Cricket balls are soft enough | Only batters can get hit
test: apparently a boy hung on the basketball hoop and the whole thing came down on him. what's the true lesson? | Don't hang on the rim; the hoop or you can fall | Hang only on outdoor hoops, they're stronger | Hang with one hand, not two | Hoops are built to be hung on, so he was just unlucky
test: my mate says a rugby kid broke his neck from a high tackle and now high tackles are banned. what's the real lesson? | Tackle below the shoulders; high tackles hurt heads and necks | High tackles are fine in practice | Tackle with your head down | Only adults get neck injuries
exam: Identify the direction in which the ball may be passed in rugby. | Backwards or sideways | Forwards only | Upwards only | In any direction at will
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the landing after a rebound jump in basketball? | Both feet are used with the knees bent | One lands on one foot with a straight leg | One lands on the toes with locked knees | One lands on the hands
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice regarding jewellery in sport. | Rings, earrings and necklaces are removed before sport | Jewellery may be worn under the shirt | Only expensive jewellery is removed | Jewellery protects against knocks
exam: Ascertain the reason for which chlorine is placed in swimming pool water. | To kill germs | To warm the water | To make the water blue | To help swimmers float
